    "source_file": "07. LECTURE NOTES - SOC SCI (GEN INFO).pdf",
    "source_type": "pdf",
    "source_path": "/Users/lyndon/All reviewers/-LET REVIEWER 2026/C. MAJOR SUBJECTS/F. SOCIAL SCIENCE - SOCIAL STUDIES/Essential Reviewers/07. LECTURE NOTES - SOC SCI (GEN INFO).pdf",
    "exam_scope": "secondary",
    "subject_slug": "major",
    "topic_slug": "social-studies-araling-panlipunan",
    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: LECTURE NOTES SOC SCI (GEN INFO) (Part 2)",
    "body": "Source: 07. LECTURE NOTES - SOC SCI (GEN INFO).pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nThe power of the President of the Philippines to\ndisapprove bills passed by Congress that he/she\nconsiders not favorable to the people is called Veto\npower.\nDue process of law means that the accused is\npresumed innocent until proven guilty.\nThe rights of private of individuals to secure for\nthemselves the enjoyable of their means of\nhappiness such as the right to a name, the right to\nform a family, and the right to security of personal\npapers and effects are called Civil rights.\nThe Bill of Rights of the Philippine Constitution does\nnot allow involuntary servitude except punishment\nfor a crime whereof the party shall have been duly\nconvicted.\nElective or appointive public officials or employees\ncannot accept any present, compensation, office or\ntitle of any kind from any foreign government\nwithout the consent of the Congress.\nThe Senate has the sole power to hear and decide\nall cases of impeachment.\nThe right of the state to take private property for\npublic use upon payment of just compensation to\nthe owner is known as Eminent domain.\nPerpetual disqualification from holding public office\ncan be considered as an accessory penalty of an\nimpeachment case.\nThe red color of the Philippine flag represent Filipino\nvalor.\nThe Philippines is a democratic and republican state.\nConsequently, sovereignty resides in the people or\ncitizenry.\nFilipino and English are the official languages of the\nPhilippines.\nA voter is disqualified from exercising his/her right to\nsuffrage is he/she has been a resident of the\nPhilippines for less than 6 months.\nA police officer who detains a suspect longer than\nwhat is required by law is liable for illegal detention.\nThe Vice President may be appointed as member of\nthe Cabinet without needing confirmation from the\nCommission on Appointments.\nThe power of the president to disapprove a bill\npassed by Congress due to allegations that said bill\nwas a hasty and ill-considered legislation is known\nas Veto power.\nJurisdiction refers to the power and authority of a\ncourt to hear, try and decide and case.\nOur constitution provides that no person shall be\ncompelled to be a witness against oneself. This\nprovision is known as the right against selfincrimination.\nAny private individual who enters an abode against\nthe will of the owner and without proper court order\nshall be guilty of trespassing.\nThe right to privacy of a person is defined as one’s\nright to be free from unwarranted publicity.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEvery defendant in a criminal case has a right to\ncounsel chiefly because the law is concerned in\nseeing that very defendant has adequate\nopportunities to present his/her case.\nA presidential candidate must reside in the\nPhilippines for at least ten (10) years before running\npublic office.\nThe following are manifestations of the sovereignty\nof the people: (1) Define the jurisdiction of their\ngovernment, (2) Define the powers of their\ngovernment, (3) Change their government, (4)\nCriticize their government.\nThe following are the powers of the Congress: (1)\nDeclare executive acts as unconstitutional, (2)\nOverride the veto power of the President, (3)\nRevoke the proclamation of martial law, (4)\nPrescribe the qualifications of judges of lower\ncourts.\nA member or the House of Representatives is\ndisqualified to serve for more than three consecutive\nterms.\nOnly natural-born citizens of the Philippines are\nqualified for government positions except for\ngovernor.\nRecords or documents within the concept of\nestablished privilege and are not made available to\nany requesting public except financial transactions.\nThe following are rights of a person under\ninvestigation for allegedly committing an offense:\n(1) To be informed of the person’s right to remain\nsilent, (2) To be informed of the nature and cause of\naccusations against the person, (3) To be provided\nwith a free counsel and (4) To have a competent\nand independent counsel preferably of the person’s\nown choice.\nNo person shall be deprived of his/her property\nwithout due process. This means that seizure by the\ngovernment of any private property should always\nbe in accordance with the law.\nA pact between or among two or more states\nintended to create a binding of rights and\nobligations upon the parties is known as a treaty.\nCultural rights ensure the well-being of the individual\nand foster the preservation, enrichment, and\ndynamic evolution of national culture based on the\nprinciple of unity in diversity in climate of free\nartistic and intellectual expression.\nFor expeditious proceeding of documents, except\notherwise provided by law or regulation, three (3)\ninitials or signatures must be contained in any\nwritten decision.\nThe people may propose amendments through\ninitiative at least ten (10) years after the\nConstitution has been ratified.\nThe general compulsory retirement age for civil\nservants is 65 years old.\n\nDR. CARL E. BALITA REVIEW CENTER TEL. NO. 735-4098/7350740\n\n-2-\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
    "material_type": "notes",
    "is_premium": false
