n\nGOCCs AND\nSUCs\n\nGOVERNMENT-OWNED AND/OR -CONTROLLED CORPORATIONS\n\nGovernment-owned and/or -controlled corporations (GOCCs), as defined\nby Presidential Decree No. 2029 (February 1986) and Memorandum Order\nNo. 64 (1993), are agencies organized as stock or non-stock corporations,\nvested with functions relating to public needs, whether governmental or\nproprietary in nature, and owned by the Government of the Philippines\ndirectly or through its instrumentalities. Established to engage in either\ncommercial or non-commercial activities, such agencies may be wholly-owned\nby the government or, in the case of stock corporations, the government has\ndirect or indirect ownership of the majority of its capital stock. Some GOCCs\nhave been created by special laws and most have their own charter defining\ntheir governance and responsibilities.\nBy virtue of R.A. No. 10149 (GOCC Governance Act of 2011), GOCCs,\nexcept for the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, state universities and colleges,\ncooperatives, local water districts, economic zone authorities and research\ninstitutions, are monitored by the Governance Commission for GOCCs\n(GCG). The GCG serves as the central advisory and oversight body to\ndetermine the relevence and evaluate the performance of these\ncorporations. It also has the power and function to ascertain whether or not\nthey should be reorganized, merged, streamlined, abolished, or privatized.\nSTATE UNIVERSITIES AND COLLEGES\n\nState universities and colleges (SUCs) are public higher education institutions\nthat have been created or organized by an act of Congress.\nAs of September 17, 2020, the Commission on Higher Education lists a total\nof 112 SUCs, with 533 satellite campuses.\nNote: Data taken from CHED official website.\n\nGOVERNMENT-OWNED\nAND/OR-CONTROLLED CORPORATIONS\n\nALEX P. BANGCOLA\nChairperson and CEO\n\nVINCENT STEVE P. BADONG\nPresident and CEO\n\nAL-AMANAH ISLAMIC INVESTMENT BANK OF THE PHILIPPINES\nDBP Remittance Center, G/F DBP 