sas olu\np\nAn ies ther\nn\nb\nem at rea\nbe t t si\nr\np\nise , G pu\nen\na rev\nSe defe egi\nto\nb\npt\nto ﬂee upri\ncu no\nep\nKa ates s re\nM\nOc\nd\nSe\nOc\noc in\ne\nn\nc\ni\nm\ndi\nab eco\nb\n\nal\nf\ner\nas\ngo ,\nen arge\nn\ni\nm\nG\ns\nt\nt\ns\nl\no\nd\nn\n4\n14 es ge’\nun roa )\n4 T ide a\nr1\ner giv eor tion\nFo s, C lavia\nr 1 res aki\nb\nbe rian\n1\ni\ne\nK d G al\ns\nb\nm\nr\nb d p lov\nm\nr\na\nU\no\ne\nm e\nc\nais\ns\nn\nbe f Se Yug\nce rit\noy co\nve ct ho\nm\nDe ion i o Ll ing\nDe utho nio P ted\nce m o es (\nt\nNo k ele zec\nt\nn\ne\na do ina\nc ty er\nD gdo ven\ni\ns\nry of C\nl’s\nele jori gov\nsa\nin Slo\nga nt S ssas\na\na\nk\nu\nt\ne\nm\nd\nM\nr id\na\nn\no\na\nP res\np\n\n353\n\n1914 -2 011\n\nT E C H N O LO G Y A N D S U P E R P O W E R S\n\nRiﬂe with bayonet\nretractable\nbayonet\n\nITALY\n\nBolt-action M91 Carcano riﬂes and carbines armed\nthe Italian infantry in World War I. This carbine has its\nbayonet ﬁxed, for use in trench ﬁghting in close quarters.\ndrum-pan magazine\nstores ammunition\n\nbutt\n\nLewis gun\nBRITAIN\n\nantiseptics and\npainkillers\n\nOriginally an American\ndesign, the Lewis gun was\nadopted by the British Army\nas its standard light machine\ngun in 1915. It armed aircraft\nand tanks, as well as infantry.\n\nsteel water jacket\ncools gun barrel\n\nlist of contents\nof pouch\n\nFirst-aid pouch\nMaxim machine gun\n\nGERMANY\n\nGERMANY\n\nGerman medical orderlies\ncarried a pouch containing basic\npainkillers and antiseptics, such\nas iodine, to treat wounded men\nbefore they were sent to\ndressing stations.\n\nThe German Army’s heavy machine\ngun, the Maschinengewehr ‘08, was\nderived from the gun invented by\nAmerican Hiram Maxim in 1884.\nIt could ﬁre 400 rounds a minute.\n\nleather\npouch\n\nWORLD WAR I\nMASS-PRODUCED WEAPONRY ALLOWS THE SLAUGHTER OF MILLIONS\n\nWorld War I has been described as “industrial warfare” as\nmanpower and economic resources of industrialized states\nwere mobilized for fighting. Modern firearms provided\narmies with firepower on an unprecedented scale.\n\nbarrel\n\nFormidable defensive systems of trenches,\nbarbed wire, and machine-gun posts made offensive\noperations costly and tended to lead to stalemate.\nWeapons used in trench warfare ranged from\ngrenades and ﬂamethrowers to homemade clubs\nand knives. From 1916, the ﬁrst slow and unreliable\ntanks made their appearance. Aircraft added a new\ndimension to warfare, carrying out reconnaissance\nand bombing, and straﬁng ground targets.\n\nbrake\n\nArtillery shells\n\n354\n\nFRANCE\n\nHowitzer\n\nArtillery ammunition in World\nWar I ranged from shrapnel to\nhigh-explosive and gas shells.\nThese shells were ﬁred by the\nFrench 75-mm ﬁeld gun.\n\nBRITAIN\n\nwheeled gun\ncarriage\n\nHowitzers such as this 6-inch British gun\nwere effective in trench warfare because\nthey launched a shell on a high trajectory,\ndropping onto the concealed enemy.\n\nW O R L D WA R I\nOfﬁcer’s compass\nBRITAIN\n\nhandset\n\nA compass was vital on a night patrol or raid.\nWithout it, soldiers could lose their way in\nthe no man’s land between the trenches,\nrendered featureless by shelling.\n\nwooden box\n\nmother-of-pearl face\ncatches the light\n\nadjustable\neye pieces\n\nscrew-on metal\nﬁlter canister\n\nGas mask\n\nFolding shovel\n\nNail club\n\nGERMANY\n\nITALY\n\nBRITAIN\n\nEffective masks were\ndeveloped that protected the\neyes and face from contact\nwith poison gas and, through\na ﬁlter respirator, neutralized\nthe gas for breathing.\n\nFor an infantryman, a shovel\nwas essential equipment,\nneeded to dig trenches or\ntemporary shelters. This\nfolding shovel was used\nby Italian alpine troops.\n\nPrimitive wooden clubs, with\nnails or other metal objects\nat the striking end, were\nused by soldiers on both\nsides as a silent, deadly\nweapon in trench raids.\n\nStereoscopic periscope\n\nField telephone\n\nGERMANY\n\nGERMANY\n\nSoldiers in trenches used\nperiscopes to keep watch on\nthe enemy line. Snipers quickly\npicked off men who exposed\ntheir heads above the parapet.\n\nAlthough radios were also used, ﬁeld\ntelephones were the main communications\nlink in trench warfare. Where telephone cables\nhad been ripped up by shelling, runners\ncarried messages to the front line by hand.\n\nleather\nbalaclava\nleather face\nmask\n\nAviator’s headgear\nBRITAIN\n\nFlying in open-cockpit\naircraft, many aviators in\nWorld War I wore leather\nbalaclavas and face masks\nto protect themselves\nagainst the cold and wind.\n\nTurkish bayonet and grenade\nTURKEY\n\nsteel canister\n\nThe Turkish Army in World War I had some obsolete\nequipment, such as swords and bayonets, but also\nstate-of-the-art German-supplied weaponry such\nas fragmentation grenades.\n\nbarrel could ﬁre\n1-pound shell\n\nleather\nskull cap\n\nAnti-aircraft gun\nBRITAIN\n\nArmies adapted existing guns,\nﬁring time-fused explosive\nshells for air defense. This\nBritish “pom-pom” gun,\nmounted on a pedestal,\nwas used in defense of\nLondon against air attack.\n\nTank helmet\nBRITAIN\n\nDesert shoes\nBRITAIN\n\nBritish troops ﬁghting against the Turks in\nthe Palestine campaign sometimes wore\nwire sand shoes over their army boots to\nfacilitate marching on desert sands.",
