W\nm\nt\nc\nin\nve tta\nNo n a ng W\nio ni\nn\nn\nU gi\nbe\n\n5 e\nr 1 vi d\nbe mo in\nm of e W\ne\nc er\nth\nDe emi ith\nPr ne W\nGo\n\nan\nm\ner Gr af\nG\n7 ip\ner\nr 1 sh aft e\nbe ttle tled Plat r\nm a\nt\nce t b cu er rlie\nDe cke ee s f Riv s ea\npo Sp e o day\nttl ur\nBa fo\n\n387\n\nSWEDE\nN\n\nNO\nRW\nAY\n\na\nB\n\nNETHERLANDS\n\nt\nal\n\nLITHUANIA\n\nAT\n\nLondon\n\nMinsk\nJun 22-29, 1941\n\nSWITZERLAND\n\nAUSTRIA\n\nBudapest\n\nOdessa\n\nSevastapol\nOct 30, 1941-Jul 4, 1942\n\nSPAIN\n\nd\n\nALGERIA\n\nGerman expansion\nBetween September 1939 and August 1942 Nazi\nGerman forces conquered most of mainland Europe,\nseizing control of an area from Norway to Crete, and\nfrom France to the Black Sea. Britain remained free of\nGerman domination, but was subjected to air attacks.\nNazi U-boats also preyed on Britain's shipping lanes.\n\ni t\n\nTUNISIA\n\nYUGOSLAVIA\n\nLY\nITA\n\nM e\n\nROC\nCO\n\nROMANIA\n\nGenoa\n\nRome\n\nMO\n\nBULGARIA\n\nack\n\nSea\n\nALBANIA\n\nr\n\nr\n\na\n\nn\n\nGREECE\n\ne\n\nAthens\n\na\n\nn\n\nKEY\n\nS e a\n\nGerman advance\n\nCrete\nMay 20-Jun 1, 1941\n\nMajor battle\nMajor German air attack\nMajor Allied air attack\n\nother\ncountries\n\nChina\n\nILL\n\nBI\n\nLL\n\ndefeat, the German army swept south to Crete and\nintervened in North Africa. Hitler’s invasion of the\nSoviet Union in June 1941 brought German forces\nto the gates of Moscow and Leningrad. But the\nGerman drive eastward came to a catastrophic\nend at Stalingrad in late 1942.\nDefeated in North Africa, the Germans then\nfaced an Allied invasion of Italy. In summer 1944,\nthe Allies landed in Normandy and broke out to\nliberate France and Belgium. A ﬁnal German\ncounterattack in the Ardennes was repulsed, and\nGermany was ﬁnally invaded from east and west.\n\n2\n\nThe German invasion of Poland in September 1939 provoked Britain and\nFrance to declare war on Germany. At first the Germans won an astonishing\nseries of victories, but from 1942 onward they were overwhelmed by the\ncombined strength of the US, the Soviet Union, and Britain.\n\n3.\n\nEUROPE\n\nION\n2 .6 BILL\nION\nN\nIO\nB\n1.6\n\nFrance\n\nTHE ALLIED BATTLE TO TURN BACK THE TIDE OF NAZI CONTROL\n\nAfter the rapid defeat of Poland in April 1940,\nthe Germans invaded Denmark and Norway.\nTheir devastating campaign swiftly overran the\nNetherlands, Belgium, and France. British Forces\nescaped from Europe through an evacuation from\nDunkirk in France. The Luftwaffe failed to\novercome the RAF in the Battle of Britain in\nsummer 1940, but British cities were subjected\nto night bombing raids during the Blitz.\nThe entry of Italy into the war in June 1940\nspread the ﬁghting to the Mediterranean and\nNorth Africa. Needing to rescue Italian forces from\n\nBl\n\nTURKEY\n\nSicily\n\ne\n\nTHE WAR IN\n\n388\n\nea\n\nS\n\nPOR\nTU\nGA\nL\n\nHUNGARY\nMilan\n\nspian\n\nSLO\nVAKIA\n\nMunich\n\nTurin\n\nStalingrad\nAug 23, 1942-Feb 2, 1943\n\nKiev\nAug 22-Sep 26, 1941\n\nCa\n\nFRANCE\n\nMoscow\nOct 2, 1941-Jan 7, 1942\n\nLATVIA\n\nEAST\nDanzig PRUSSIA\nHamburg\nRotterdam\nPlymouth\nBerlin\nEssen\nDunkirk\nWarsaw\nCologne\nMay 26-Jun 4, 1940\nSedan BELGIUM\nSep 8-28, 1939\nGERMANY\nMay 12-15, 1940\nLUXEMBOURG\nPrague\nPOLAND\nParis\n\nBay of\nBiscay\n\nUSSR\n\nESTONIA\n\nSe\n\nHull\nCoventry\n\nTI\nN\nLA\n\nDENMARK\n\nUNITED\nKINGDOM\n\nBelfast\n\nLiverpool\nBirmingham\nCardiff\n\nLeningrad\nSep 8, 1941-Jan 27, 1944\n\nic\n\nIRELAND\n\nC\n\nO\n\nAN\n\nD\n\nNorth\nSea\n\nGlasgow\n\nCE\n\nFINLAN\n\n11.3\nBILLION\n\nSoviet\nUnion\n\n31.4\nBILLION\n\nUK\n\nUS lend-lease\nThe US provided its Allies with vast quantities\nof war equipment and supplies, under the\nlend-lease program. Britain and the Soviet\nUnion were the major beneﬁciaries.\n\nEUROPE IN 1942 By 1942 Nazi Germany\ndominated mainland Europe. Its allies and\nsatellites included Italy, Hungary, Bulgaria,\nSlovakia, Croatia, and Vichy France. Greater\nGermany expanded before and during the war\nto include Austria, French Alsace-Lorraine,\nand much of Poland (which ceased to exist as a\nstate in October 1939). Although the Nazis\nfound some willing collaborators, their\nambition to found a “New Order” in Europe\neventually came to nothing.\n\nSWEDE\nN\n\nNO\nRW\nAY\n\nN\n\nUSSR\nLA\n\nSe\n\nDENMARK\n\nic\n\nN\n\nTI\n\nND\n\nNorth\nSea\n\nAT\n\nLA\n\nIRE\n\nal\nB\n\nUNITED NETHERLANDS\nKINGDOM\nLondon\n\nArnhem\nSep 17-26, 1944\n\nEAST LITHUANIA\nPRUSSIA\n\nPOR\nTU\nGA\nL\n\nM e d\n\nBl\n\nack\n\nAllied offensives\nThe tide of war turned\nwith the British victory at\nEl Alamein in North Africa\nand the Soviet triumph at\nStalingrad. Italy surrendered\nin 1943 after the Allies landed,\nand after the Normandy\ninvasion on D-day in 1944,\nGermany was crushed\nbetween Soviet forces\nadvancing from the east and\nthe Western Allies attacking\nfrom the west.\n\nTURKEY\nMessina\n\ne\n\nALBANIA\n\nr a\n\nSicily\n\nALGERIA\n\nGREECE\n\nn e\n\nTUN\n\na\n\nKEY\n\nIS\n\nAllied advance\n\nn\n\nS e a\n\nEl Alamein\nOct 23 -Nov 5, 1942\n\nI\n\nA\n\nMajor battle\nMajor Allied\nﬁrebomb attack\n\nea\n\nBULGARIA\n\nSalerno\nSep 9, 1943\n\ni t\n\nS\n\nSea\n\nr\n\nOCCO\n\nMONTENEGRO\n\nRome\n\nCassino\nJan 17-May 18, 1944\n\nspian\n\nROMANIA\n\nCROATIA\nSERBIA\n\nLY\nITA\n\nAnzio\nJan 22-Jun 5, 1944\n\nCa\n\nHUNGARY\n\nAUSTRIA",
