ations.\nBritain did not hesitate to ally\nitself with the Soviet Union,\ndespite Prime Minister Winston\n\nChurchill’s (see 1940) strong\ndislike of Soviet communism.\nBut the British really needed\nthe US to enter the war.\nPresident Roosevelt (see 1933)\nmade no pretense of neutrality. In\nMarch, he introduced Lend-Lease\nto supply Britain with military\nequipment paid for by the US\ngovernment. American shipyards\nand factories beneﬁted greatly\nfrom this, as did American workers\nwith plentiful and well-paid jobs.\nLater in the year, free military\naid from the US was extended to\nthe Soviet Union.\nIn August, Roosevelt and\nChurchill met at Placentia Bay\nin Newfoundland, Canada, where\nthey signed the Atlantic Charter,\na statement of joint war aims\nembodying liberal democratic\nprinciples. American warships\nwere already escorting convoys\nin the eastern Atlantic, and in\nOctober a US destroyer was\nsunk by a German torpedo, but\nFighting in the snow\nA German soldier experiencing the\nRussian winter during the invasion\nof the Soviet Union. Axis forces\nwere ill-equipped to cope with the\nconditions.\n\ns\nke ity\nta ibil\nS\ns\n7 U on f\nain\nUS\nly esp e o rit\nle\nt 1 an\nJu er r enc m B\ns\nsa\nu\nf\ns\nov de d fro\nug ose o on an\nr\nA\nn\ng\np\np\nfo la\nim bar o Ja\nIce\nem oil t\nof\n\nd\n9\nst t an\ngu vel ign er,\nAu ose ill s art\nh\nRo urc ic Ch t of\nCh lant men\nAt tate ms\na s r ai\nwa\n\nxis\n\nA\nr8 e\nbe plet f\nem m t o\npt co en\nSe ces lem\nr\nfo circ rad\nen ning\nLe\n\nRoosevelt felt he lacked the\npopular support needed for\na declaration of war.\nRoosevelt’s dilemma was\nresolved by the Japanese.\nThe US opposed Japan’s\nexpansion into Asia, and\nafter Japanese troops\nentered French Indochina\nin July Roosevelt imposed\nan oil embargo. Since Japan\nwas entirely dependent on\nimported oil, its government had\nthe choice of abandoning its\nmilitary ambitions or ﬁghting a\nwar with the US.\nFollowing a plan advocated by\nAdmiral Isoroku Yamamoto, on\n\nISOROKU YAMAMOTO\n(1884-1943)\nIn the 1930s, Japanese\nAdmiral Isoroku Yamamoto\nbecame a leading advocate\nof naval air power. As naval\ncommander-in-chief he was\nthe architect of the raid on\nPearl Harbor in 1941. He\nwas killed in April 1943 when\nhis aircraft, identiﬁed by\nAmerican intelligence, was\nshot down over Bougainville\nIsland in the Paciﬁc.\n\nnd\n,\ns\nLe\nce oon ow\ns\nr\niet\nd\nfo ph sc\nn\nov or\nA\nen\nxt Unio\nxis n Ty Mo\n6 S maj\n19 re\nev\ne\ni\nA\nr\nr\nd\no\nt\nar\nbe nch sive\nUS vie\nbe ptu al, K\nr 2 ati\nbe per tow\n30 So\nem lau ffen nt\nem s ca apit\no\nt\nc\nr\ne\nt\no\nO\nr\np\nt\nc\nDe ces r-o fro\nbe\nOc gin nsiv\nSe ade ian\nto aid\nfor unte scow\nbe offe\nOc ase\ninv rain\nn\nk\ne\nco Mo\na\nL\nU\non\nxis",
