    "body": "Source: Timelines of History-The Ultimate Visual Guide by Smithsonian.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nThe world is richer than ever, strengthened by international\nalliances and technology, but there is still widespread\npoverty. Wars continue to be fought, and even developed\nnations can be devastated by natural disasters.\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\n0 10 20 30 40 50 60\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nThere are currently almost 7 billion people living on the planet,\nthree times the population of 1900. The fate of each person\ndepends on where they live, and the distribution of the world’s wealth has\nchanged little since World War II. Many high-income countries are in the\nnorthern hemisphere, with the world’s poorest in sub-Saharan Africa. The six\nwealthiest countries account for more than half the world's Gross Domestic\nProduct (GDP)-the value of all the goods and services a country produces-\nwhile over half the world’s population live on less than $2.50 a day.\nThe countries of Western Europe and North America have well-established\nbusiness sectors, with multinational corporations selling products globally. The\nnations of Africa and Central America have a smaller range of industries, and\nmany depend on trading a single commodity. But this is slowly changing, and the\neconomies of countries such as Mexico, Brazil, and India are growing rapidly.\n\nATLANTIC\nOCEAN\n\nNORTH\nAMERICA\n\nInterdependent world\nThe economies of the world are based\non a vast range of industries, and\npopulations range from 800 (Vatican\nCity) to over a billion (India and China).\nNo countries are fully self-supporting,\nhowever, and they all depend on trade\nwith other countries to meet their\nneeds fully.\n\nGDP PER CAPITA IN US DOLLARS (BILLIONS)\n\n0\n\n40,000\n\n60,000\n\nQatar\nLiechtenstein\nLuxembourg\nBermuda\nNorway\nSingapore\nJersey\nKuwait\nBrunei\nUS\n\n70,000\n\n80,000\n\n90,000\n\n140,000\n\n150,000\n\nIn the money\nWhen a country's wealth is divided\nby the number of people who live\nin it, Qatar emerges as the richest\nnation in the world.\n\nSOUTH\nAMERICA\n\nPACIFIC\nOCEAN\nBRAZIL\n\nGROWTH RATE\n\n15%\n14%\n13%\n\nOn the up\nThe fastestgrowing economies\nare mainly in\ndeveloping\ncountries.\n\n12%\n10%\n9%\n8%\n\n0\n\nGhana\nQatar\nTurkmenistan\nChina\nLiberia\nIndia\nAngola\nIraq\nEthiopia\nMozambique\n\n7%\n\n800\n700\n\nOut of pocket\nThe poorest\ncountries in the\nworld are all in\nsub-Saharan Africa.\n\n600\n\nBrazil has the largest economy in South\nAmerica, and the ninth largest in the\nworld. Well-established agriculture,\nmining, and service sectors have helped\ncreate a healthy economy, and Brazil is\nalso rich in natural resources. Huge gaps\nremain between rich and poor, however.\nMale\n\n500\n400\n300\n0\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\nMalawi\nSierra Leone\nNiger\nEritrea\nAfrican Republic\nSomalia\nLiberia\nZimbabwe\nCongo\nBurundi\n\n20%\n\n900\nGDP IN US DOLLARS (BILLIONS)\n\n21%\n\n40 30 20 10\n\n0\n\n0\n\n10 20 30 40\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\n466\n\nATLANTIC\nOCEAN\n\nT H E G LO B A L E C O N O M Y\nGERMANY\n\nRUSSIA\n\nGermany reacted to the Male\nunion of East and West\nGermany by becoming\nthe largest economy on\nthe European continent,\nand the ﬁfth wealthiest\nin the world by GDP.\nGermany is the world’s\nsecond-largest trader in\nimports and exports.\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n\nThe world’s largest\ncountry, Russia, has seen\nhuge changes since the\nfall of the Soviet Union.\nIt has vast natural\nresources, such as oil\nand gas, and is the\nworld’s largest oil\nproducer, but many\nRussian people still\nlive in poverty.\n\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\n40 30 20 10\n\n0\n\n0\n\nMale\n\n40 30 20 10\n\n10 20 30 40\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\n0\n\n0\n\n10 20 30 40\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nCHINA\nChina, the world’s most populated country, has\none of the fastest-growing economies and is\nthe top exporter. Factories and mines produce\nmost of China's wealth, but in the future this\nmay be slowed by an aging population.\n\nEUROPE\n\nA\n\nMed\n\ni terra\n\nnean Se\n\nS\n\nI\n\nA\n\nMale\n\na\n\nT\n\nhe\n\nGu\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\nlf\n\nRe\ndS\n\nArabian\nSea\n\n60 50 40 30 20 10 0\n\n0 10 20 30 40 50 60\n\nea\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nINDIA\nIt is estimated that\nIndia’s economy will\ngrow faster than any other country's over\nthe next two decades. It has a young and\ngrowing workforce, has opened up foreign\ntrade, and hosts millions of entrepreneurs\nrunning small, but successful, businesses.\nMale\n\nA\n\nP\n\nA F R I C A\n\n60 50 40 30 20 10 0\n\nIF\n\nIC\n\nOC\n\nEAN\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\nSOUTH AFRICA\n\nC\n\nAUSTRALIA\n\n0 10 20 30 40 50 60\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nAGE\nMale\nFemale\nSouth Africa has\n100+\none of the largest\n95-99\n90-94\neconomies on the\n85-89\n80-84\nAfrican continent,\n75-79\nbut poverty is\n70-74\n65-69\nwidespread. One\n60-64\n55-59\nin seven people\n50-54\n45-49\nis infected with\n40-44\nHIV/AIDS, high\n35-39\n30-34\ncrime and land\n25-29\n20-24\nredistribution\n15-19\n10-14\nremain an issue,\n5-9\n0-4\nand unemployment\nis high.\n40 30 20 10 0\n0 10 20 30 40\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\nMale\n\nFemale\n\nAGE\n\nAUSTRALIA\nAustralia’s economy stands out because\nit survived the global downturn without\ngoing into recession. It has a strong\nservice-providing economy as well\nas healthy exports of agriculture\nand mining products. Natural gas\ndeposits are evenly spread throughout\n40 30 20 10 0\nthe continent.\n\n100+\n95-99\n90-94\n85-89\n80-84\n75-79\n70-74\n65-69\n60-64\n55-59\n50-54\n45-49\n40-44\n35-39\n30-34\n25-29\n20-24\n15-19\n10-14\n5-9\n0-4\n\n0\n\n10 20 30 40\n\nPOPULATION (IN MILLIONS)\n\n467\n\nD I R EC T O RY\n\nRULERS AND LEADERS\n\nDIRECTORY\nRULERS AND LEADERS\nWhether leadership is achieved through heredity, democracy, or sheer brute\nforce, leaders make decisions that determine how history will judge their time\nin power. Great leaders have been the salvation of their people, while weak\nleaders have been responsible for bringing mighty empires to their knees.\n\nPERIOD\n\nNew Kingdom\n(1550-1069 BCE)\n\nDYNASTY\n\nNOTABLE PHARAOHS\n\n17th Dynasty (at Thebes)\n(1650-1550 BCE)\n\nKamose (1555-1550 BCE)\n\n18th Dynasty\n(1550-1295 BCE)",
