    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: MINDGYM SOCIAL STUDIES FALLACIES LECTURE NOTES (Part 1)",
    "body": "Source: 1. MINDGYM_SOCIAL STUDIES-FALLACIES_LECTURE NOTES.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nFALLACIES\nSocial studies\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nFALLACIES\n1. Fallacies of Relevance\n2. Fallacies of Presumption\n\n3. Fallacies of Ambiguity\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nFALLACIES OF RELEVANCE\n1. ad baculum - Appeal to Force\n2. ad misericordiam - Appeal to Pity\n3. ad populum - Appeal to Emotion\n4. ad verecundiam - Appeal to Authority\n5. ad hominem - Mirror-image: appeal to authority\n\n6. ad ignoratium - Appeal to Ignorance\n7. ignoratio elenchi - Irrelevant Conclusion\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD BACULUM - APPEAL TO FORCE\nIn the appeal to force, someone in a position of power\nthreatens to bring down unfortunate consequences upon\nanyone who dares to disagree with a proffered proposition.\nAlthough it is rarely developed so explicitly, a fallacy of this\ntype might propose:\nIf you do not agree with my political opinions, you will receive a\ngrade of F for this course.\nI believe that Herbert Hoover was the greatest President of the\nUnited States.\nTherefore, Herbert Hoover was the greatest President of the\nUnited States.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD MISERICORDIAM - APPEAL TO PITY\nTurning this on its head, an appeal to pity tries to win\nacceptance by pointing out the unfortunate consequences\nthat will otherwise fall upon the speaker and others, for\nwhom we would then feel sorry.\nI am a single parent, solely responsible for the financial\nsupport of my children.\nIf you give me this traffic ticket, I will lose my license and be\nunable to drive to work\n\nIf I cannot work, my children and I will become homeless and\nmay starve to death.\nTherefore, you should not give me this traffic ticket.\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD POPULUM - APPEAL TO EMOTION\nIn a more general fashion, the appeal to emotion\nrelies upon emotively charged language to arouse\nstrong feelings that may lead an audience to\naccept its conclusion:\nAs all clear-thinking residents of our fine state have already\nrealized, the Governor's plan for financing public education\nis nothing but the bloody-fanged wolf of socialism cleverly\ndisguised in the harmless sheep's clothing of concern for\nchildren.\nTherefore, the Governor's plan is bad public policy.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD VERECUNDIAM\n\nAPPEAL TO AUTHORITY\nIn an appeal to authority, the opinion of someone\nfamous or accomplished in another area of\nexpertise is supposed to guarantee the truth of a\nconclusion. Thus, for example:\nFederal Reserve Chair Alan Greenspan believes that\nspiders are insects.\n\nTherefore, spiders are insects.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD HOMINEM\nThe mirror-image of the appeal to authority is the ad hominem\nargument, where we are encouraged to reject a proposition because it is\nthe stated opinion of someone regarded as disreputable in some way.\nThis can happen in several different ways, but all involve the claim that\nthe proposition must be false because of who believes it to be true:\n\nHarold maintains that the legal age for drinking beer should\nbe 18 instead of 21.\nBut we all know that Harold . . .\n. . . dresses funny and smells bad. or\n. . . is 19 years old and would like to drink legally or\n. . . believes that the legal age for voting should be 21, not 18 or\n. . . doesn't understand the law any better than the rest of us.\nTherefore, the legal age for drinking beer should be 21 instead of 18\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAD IGNORATIAM - APPEAL TO\nIGNORANCE\nAn appeal to ignorance proposes that we accept\nthe truth of a proposition unless an opponent can\nprove otherwise. Thus, for example:\nNo one has conclusively proven that there is no intelligent\nlife on the moons of Jupiter.\nTherefore, there is intelligent life on the moons of Jupiter.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nFALLACIES OF PRESUMPTION\n1. Accident\n2. Converse Accident\n3. False Cause\n4. Begging the Question\n5. Complex Question\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nACCIDENT\nThe fallacy of accident begins with the statement\nof some principle that is true as a general rule, but\nthen errs by applying this principle to a specific\ncase that is unusual or atypical in some way.\nWomen earn less than men earn for doing thesame\nwork.\nOprah Winfrey is a woman.\n\nTherefore, Oprah Winfrey earns less than male talkshow hosts.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nCONVERSE ACCIDENT\nThe fallacy of converse accident begins with a\nspecific case that is unusual or atypical in some\nway, and then errs by deriving from this case the\ntruth of a general rule.\nDennis Rodman wears earrings and is an excellent\nrebounder.\nTherefore, people who wear earrings are excellent\nrebounders.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nFALSE CAUSE\nThe fallacy of false cause infers the presence of a\ncausal connection simply because events appear\nto occur in correlation or (in the post hoc, ergo\npropter hoc variety) temporal succession.\nThe moon was full on Thursday evening.\nOn Friday morning I overslept.\nTherefore, the full moon caused me to oversleep.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nBEGGING THE QUESTION\nBegging the question is the fallacy of using the\nconclusion of an argument as one of the premises\noffered in its own support. Although this often\nhappens in an implicit or disguised fashion, an\nexplicit version would look like this:\nAll dogs are mammals.\nAll mammals have hair.",
