    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: MINDGYM SOCIAL STUDIES MAJOR FORMS OF GOVERNMENT LECTURE NOTES (Part 1)",
    "body": "Source: 3. MINDGYM_SOCIAL STUDIES-MAJOR FORMS OF GOVERNMENT_LECTURE NOTES.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nGeneral Education\n\nSOCIAL STUDIES\nMajor Forms of\nGovernment\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n1. Unitary System\n•\n\nA central government that holds all legitimate power.\n\n•\n\nThere is little or no sphere of government autonomy to\nlocal or component units like the provinces, barangays\nand cities in the Philippines.\n\n•\n\nThe central government is given all authority to make all\npolicies, although it may deputize other governmental\nstructures to act on its behalf.\n\n•\n\nPolitical power is more centralized (concentrated ore on\ncentral authority) in unitary than federal systems.\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n1. Unitary System\n• Advantage - In this kind of government there is\nclear hierarchical authority.\n• Disadvantage - For the reason that the loyalty\nof the citizens is focused on the governmental\nauthority, citizens tend to identify with the\ncountry as a whole rather than with regional\nauthorities.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n2. Federal State\na. Local governmental authorities are set-up by the\nconstitution and these authorities are given certain\npolitical decisions over which they have a legal\nmonopoly of control.\nb. A federal government is one in which government\npowers are divided between the central\ngovernment and its local government units as\nspecified in the constitution of the state.\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n2. Federal State\nc. In contrast to a unitary state, there is an explicit\nsharing of power among levels of government\nin a federation, and no level has legal power to\ndominate any other level in policy domains.\nd. The essence of federation is coordination not\nhierarchy.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n2. Federal State\n•\n\nAdvantage - Central or local government is\nindependent in its sphere of jurisdiction.\n\n•\n\nDisadvantage - There is a duplication of\noffices and personnel in central and local\ngovernment, and to maintain this would be a\nheavy financial burden to the government.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nSystems of Government\n3. Confederation\n• This is an association wherein states delegate\ncertain power to a supranational central\ngovernment but retain primary power.\n− Examples: United Nations, European Union,\nASEAN, APEC, United Arab Emirates\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForm of Distribution\n\nStrengths\n\nWeaknesses\n\nUnitary\n\n• Clear authority\n• Decisive control\n• No stalemates\nbetween center and\nperiphery\n\n• Hyperconcentration of\npower\n• Weak representation\nof diversity and\nminorities\n\nFederal\n\n• Duplication and\n• Diversity represented\noverlap of power\n• Checks on center’s\n• Conflicts over\npower\nultimate power\n• Unity created\n• Slow process,\ncompromise\n\nConfederation\n\n• Facilitation of\ncooperation\n• Power retained by\nsub-units\n\n• Conditional\ncompliance\n• Instability\n• Limited power\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nKey Terms\n• DECENTRALIZATION - the transfer of powers from\ncentral government to lower levels in a politicaladministrative and territorial hierarchy\n• DEVOLUTION - the transfer of “natural resource\nmanagement to local individuals and institutions’\nlocated within and outside of government”\n• DECONCENTRATION - the process by which the\nagents of central government control are relocated\nand geographically dispersed\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of government\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of Government\nI. Ruled by One Person\nA. MONARCHY\nB. OLIGARCHY\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of Government\nA. MONARCHY\n•\n\nA government that has a single person who is generally\nconsidered the ruler by the title and birthright. Titles\ninclude: Czar, King, Queen, Emperor, Caesar, etc.\n\n•\n\nPower is absolute and is either taken through conquest\nor passed down to family members without regard for\nability or appropriateness.\n\n•\n\nSociety is formed around feudal groups or tribes in\nwhich the ruling family delegates power and authority\nbased upon the desires of a single individual.\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of Government\nA. MONARCHY\na. Absolute Monarchy - The monarch exercises absolute\npower and wields executive, legislative and judicial\npowers (predominated in France).\nb. Limited Monarchy - The monarch delegates powers.\nFor example, the legislative body, judicial power by the\ncourt and executive power by the King / Queen\nexercise legislative power. They rule according to their\nconstitution. (United Kingdom, Belgium, the\nNetherlands, Japan, Spain)\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of Government\nB. DICTATORSHIP\n•\n\nA type of government controlled by a single\nindividual and giving the people little or no individual\nfreedom.\n\n•\n\nTypically a person who rules by threat of force.\n\n•\n\nPeople who are loyal to a dictatorship swear\nallegiance to the person first and the country second.\n\n•\n\nFascism, Theocracies, Monarchies and\nCommunism can all be dictatorships.\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nForms of Government\nB. DICTATORSHIP\n•\n\nA Republic cannot be a dictatorship.\n\n•\n\nThis can be applicable during a period of chaos or\ndiscontent when the leader seeks emergency powers\nto implement policies.\n\n•\n\nCan also be used by a leader to remain in office and\nmaintain power.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.",
