    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: MINDGYM SOCIAL STUDIES MAJOR FORMS OF GOVERNMENT LECTURE NOTES (Part 3)",
    "body": "Source: 3. MINDGYM_SOCIAL STUDIES-MAJOR FORMS OF GOVERNMENT_LECTURE NOTES.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nAnarchy\n• A situation where there is no government.\n• This can happen after a civil war in a country,\nwhen a government has been destroyed and\nrival groups are fighting to take its place.\n• Anarchists are people who believe that\ngovernment is a bad thing in that it stops\npeople organizing their own lives\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nCapitalist\n• In a capitalist or free-market country, people\ncan own their own businesses and property.\n• People can also buy services for private use,\nsuch as healthcare.\n• But most capitalist governments also provide\ntheir own education, health and welfare\nservices.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nCommunist\n• In a communist country, the government owns\nproperty such as businesses and farms.\n• It provides its people's healthcare, education\nand welfare.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nRepublic\n• A republic is a country that has no monarch.\n• The head of the country is usually an elected\npresident.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nRevolutionary\n• If a government is overthrown by force, the\nnew ruling group is sometimes called a\nrevolutionary government.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nPresidential vs\nParliamentary\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nPresidential System\n• The executive power is exercised by a single\npresident elected by popular vote.\n• S/he holds office for a specific period of time\nfixed by the constitution of the state.\n• S/he appoints the members of his / her\ncabinet who are responsible to the president.\nTheir service is co-terminus with the\npresidents.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nPresidential System\n• An essential element of this system is the\nseparation of powers of the executive,\nlegislative, and judicial branches, which is\nmade more effective by checks and balances\nof the 3 branches.\n• This is the form of government of the United\nStates and the Philippines.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nParliamentary System\n•\n\nExecutive power belongs to the Prime Minister and his\nCabinet. They are the leaders of the majority party in\nthe Parliament and they remain in office as long as\nthey have the confidence and support of the\nparliamentary majority.\n\n•\n\nThey participate in parliamentary debates and defend\ntheir policies and bills, and see to it that they are\napproved for the nations welfare and for their\nrespective constituents, since they are also elected as\nmembers of parliament.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nParliamentary System\n• They are responsible to Parliament for their\nacts and decisions as members of the Cabinet.\n• The Prime Minister and his cabinet are\nremovable by Parliament by means of\nparliamentary censure on the members of the\ncabinet and a no-confidence vote against the\nPrime Minister.\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nParliamentary System\n1. Collective ministerial responsibility\n2. Fusion of executive and legislative power\n3. Cabinet responsibility to parliament\n4. Party discipline\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.\n\nPresidential VS Parliamentary\n\nExclusively for My Review Coach online LET review. Not for redistribution. © 2011 My Review Coach of\n\nPhilippines.",
