    "source_path": "/Users/lyndon/All reviewers/-LET REVIEWER 2026/C. MAJOR SUBJECTS/F. SOCIAL SCIENCE - SOCIAL STUDIES/Social Dimensions of Education/5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf",
    "exam_scope": "secondary",
    "subject_slug": "major",
    "topic_slug": "social-studies-araling-panlipunan",
    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills (Part 1)",
    "body": "Source: 5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nLET REVIEWER\nSocial Dimensions of Education\n1. Mr. Soriano has been is a Sociology Professor for almost a decade. In his class,\nhe asked this question: “What type of learning environment should a teacher\nimplement in a highly pluralistic society?” If you are his student, what will your\nanswer?\nA. Safe, Secure and Gender Sensitive\nB. Safe, Secure and Gender-Biased\nC. Safe and Gender-Biased\nD. Secure and Gender-Biased\nAnswer: A. The teacher should provide a learning environment that is safe, secure\nand gender-sensitive - no discrimination and other forms of oppressions.\n2. In some cultures, women never experienced to be in school. School is no longer\nnecessary for them since their essence is to serve their families and perform\ndomestic responsibilities.\nIs this CORRECT?\nA. Yes, women are domestic partners.\nB. No, there is gender equality in education.\nC. It depends on what kind of culture that a woman is accustomed to.\nD. No, women are skilful just like men.\nAnswer: B. Education is for all. This is a right of everyone and not a privilege.\n3. “Man is nature is good” according to Confucius, Rousseau and Mencius. This\ncharacteristic of man makes him easier to be _____.\nA. Motivated\nC. Rewarded\nB. Taught\nD. Respected\nAnswer: B. - Taught. The innate goodness in man is an indication of his being\nobedient. He follows because he wants to learn. Motivation, reward and respect are just\nmanifestations of learning.\n4. For Teacher Wendell, to promote fairness among his students of diverse gender,\nfamily background and culture, he should ensure a learning environment that is\nI.\nInclusive\nII.\nGender-sensitive\nIII.\nExclusive\nA. I and II\nB. I and III\n\nC. II and III\nD. I, II and III\n\nAnswer: D. a, b and c. Multicultural education embodies a perspective rather than\ncurriculum. The teacher must consider children’s cultural identities and be aware of their\nown biases.\n5. What do the school campus expressions “promdi” and “barriotic” indicate?\nA. The power of the rich\nC. the powerlessness of the poor\nB. The prevalence of ethnocentrism\nD. the low literacy rate of the country\nAnswer: C. the powerlessness of the poor. It is worth noting that education is not a\nprivilege, but a right. In education, EQUALITY is the rule.\n6. With R.A. 9155, to which body were all the functions, programs, activities of the\nDepartment of Education related to Sports competition transferred?\nA. Philippine Sports Commission\nB. National Commission for Culture and the Arts\nC. Technical Education Services Department Authority\nD. Commission on Higher Education\nAnswer: A. Philippine Sports Commission - Republic Act 9155, otherwise called\nthe Governance of Basic Education Act, was passed in August 2001, transforming the\nname of Department of Education, Culture and Sports (DECS) to the Department of\nEducation (DepEd) and redefining the role of field offices (regional offices, division\noffices, district offices and schools). It provides the overall framework for school head\nempowerment by strengthening their leadership roles and school-based management\nwithin the context of transparency and local accountability.\n7. Which pillar of learning is vital in building a genuine and lasting culture of peace in\nthe world?\nA. Learning to Do\nC. Learning to Live Together\nB. Learning to Know\nD. Learning to Be\nAnswer: C. Learning to Live Together is vital in building a genuine and lasting\nculture of peace in the world. This can be achieved by developing an understanding of\nothers and their history, traditions and spiritual values, and appreciation of their\ndependence.\n8. “The Principles of Spontaneity as against artificially will make you accomplish\nsomething. Leave nature to itself and you will live in harmony.” This is an advice\nfrom the _____.\nA. Hindu\nC. Shintoist\nB. Taoist\nD. Buddhist\n\nAnswer: B. Taoist - the concept of spontaneity is consistent with the concept of\nnaturality as expounded by Lao Tzu, founder of Taoism, in his Tai Te Ching.\n9. The government’s educational program on _____ made the Philippine Education\nPlacement Test accessible for adults and out-of-school-youth.\nA. Equitable Access\nC. Quality and Relevance\nB. Quality\nD. Relevance\nAnswer: A. Equitable Access. PEPT (Philippine Education Placement Test)\nallows out-of-school youth and others who did not finish school to have an equitable\naccess to education. For example, an out-of-school youth who only finished grade 6\nmay be granted to enrol in college of the result of his PEPT will yield a sufficient score\nthat will allow him to hurdle college education.\n10. In a multicultural education, much emphasis is given on...\nA. Perspective\nC. Achievement\nB. Curriculum\nD. Environment\nAnswer: A. Perspective. In a multicultural education, teachers must consider\nchildren’s cultural identities and be aware of their own biases.\n11. How can you best describe the role of the teacher under the Pillar of Learning to\nKnow?\nA. Guidance Counsellor\nC. Facilitator\nB. Instructor\nD. Author\nAnswer: C. Facilitator. The role of the teacher under “Learning to Know” is to be\nfacilitator, catalyst, monitor, and evaluator of learning because the process of learning to\nthink is a lifelong one and can be enhanced by every kind of human experience.\n12. What best describes the Doctrine of Salvation?\nA. Set of moral teaching and values\nB. Belief in the existence of deity\nC. Highest goal of the faithful\nD. Acts and ceremonies of religion\nAnswer: C. Highest goal of the faithful. Major religions - Christianity, Islam,\nBuddhism, and Hinduism - teach a doctrine of salvation. They stress that salvation is\nthe highest goal of the faithful and one that all followers should try to achieve. Religions\ndiffer, however, in what salvation is and how it can be gained.\n13. Which among the following is the first essential characteristic of culture?\nA. Ideational\nC. Cumulative\n\nB. Diverse\n\nD. Learned",
    "material_type": "notes",
    "is_premium": false
  },
  {
    "source_file": "5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf",
    "source_type": "pdf",
    "source_path": "/Users/lyndon/All reviewers/-LET REVIEWER 2026/C. MAJOR SUBJECTS/F. SOCIAL SCIENCE - SOCIAL STUDIES/Social Dimensions of Education/5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf",
    "exam_scope": "secondary",
    "subject_slug": "major",
    "topic_slug": "social-studies-araling-panlipunan",
    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills (Part 2)",
    "body": "Source: 5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nAnswer: D. Learned. The first essential characteristic of culture is that it is\nlearned. The child has the ability to learn behavior patterns.\n14. Which of the following is the most important component in educational reform?\nA. Student’s participation\nB. School’s good relationship with parents\nC. Parent’s involvement\nD. Teacher’s interaction with parents and students\nAnswer: C. Parent’s involvement. The Department of Education posits that every\nschool will promote partnerships that will increase parental involvement and\nparticipation in promoting the social, emotional and academic growth of children.\n15. Macky, an 8-year-old child, immediately kisses the hand of his elders whenever\nhe meets them. He learned this value by observing his elder sisters and parents.\nThis pattern of acquiring values is based on this principle.\nA. Values are intrinsically motivated.\nB. Values are taught not caught.\nC. Values are caught not taught.\nD. Values are innate.\nAnswer: C. Values are caught not taught. In the above scenario, Macky caught\nthe values from his parents.\n16. What is man’s ultimate vocation and destiny according to Paulo Freire?\nA. Rationalization\nC. Secularization\nB. Humanization\nD. Deregulation\nAnswer: B. Humanization - According to Freire this is the ultimate vocation and\ndestiny of man. This can be achieved through the process of conscientization, a\nprocess of becoming aware of the contradictions existing within oneself and in society,\nand of gradually being able to bring about personal and social transformations.\n17. Which according to RA 9155 is considered as the “heart of the formal education\nsystem”?\nA. Pupil\nC. Classroom\nB. Teacher\nD. School\nAnswer: D. School - Republic Act 9155, otherwise called the Governance of Basic\nEducation Act was Passed in August 2001 transforming the name of Department of\nEducation, Culture and Sports (DECS) to the Department of Education (DepEd) and\n\nredefining the role of field offices (regional offices, division offices, district offices and\nschool). It provides the overall framework for school based management within the\ncontext of transparency and local accountability.\n18. Teacher makes Roel full use of the question-and-answer as a model for\ndiscussion. From whom is this question-and-answer method?\nA. Kant\nC. Aristotle\nB. Socrates\nD. Plato\nAnswer: B. Socrates. - This philosopher is known for his Socratic Dialogue. This\ntechnique refers to the conversation method.\n19. Teacher Lyn knows of the illegal gambling that his neighbor is operating.\nHowever, he chooses to be quite about it for the fear getting involved in any\ninvestigation that this may cause. Which among the following principles of morality\ndoes Teacher Lyn fail to apply?\nA. Choose what is good and avoid what is evil.\nB. Choose lesser evil.\nC. The end justifies the mean.\nD. The end does not justify the mean.\nAnswer: A. Choose what is good and avoid what is evil. Ethics/Morality can be\nsummed up in doing what is right (especially if this is for the common good) and avoid\nwhat is evil. In this case, Teacher Lyn has the responsibility not only to identify this\nsocial issue but also to be involved in addressing it.\n20. Which among the following pillars of learning aimed for the holistic development\nand complete fulfilment of man?\nA. Learning to Do\nC. Learning to Be\nB. Learning to Live Together\nD. Learning to Know\nAnswer: C. Learning to Be. This pillar of education re-asserted the principle that\neducation should contribute to every person’s development. This includes not only the\nmind and the body but also the spiritual, aesthetics appreciation and sensitive aspects\nof the individual.\n21. Which among the following situations violate the principle of respect?\nI.\nTeacher Val tells her student that what teacher Max taught is wrong.\nII.\nTeacher Angelique, upon learning what Teacher Val did, asked the students\nnot to attend his class.\nIII.\nTeacher Jon is giving special favour to students to please the students so that\nhe can get a remarkable result in the evaluation.\n\nA. I and II\nB. I and III\n\nC. II and III\nD. I, II and III\n\nAnswer: D. I, II and III. As provided by the Code of Ethics for Teachers, in line\nwith RA 7836: A teacher shall at all times, be imbued with the spirit of professional\nloyalty, mutual confidence, and faith in one another, self-sacrifice for the common good\nand full cooperation with colleagues. When the best interest of the learners, the school,\nof the profession is at stake in any controversy, teachers shall support one another.\n22. What pillar of learning is concerned on the material development rather than\nhuman development?\nA. Learning to Live Together\nC. Learning to Know\nB. Learning to Be\nD. Learning to Do\nAnswer: D. Learning to Do. This pillar of learning addresses the issue of\noccupational learning. It aims to equip people for the type of work needed in the future.\nThis aim should be in line with one’s education.\n23. It is the responsibility of the teacher to promote the cultural and educational\nheritage of the nation. As such, it is her/her obligation also to convey all of these\nto the students. In this case, which among the following will make the teacher\naccomplish this obligation?\nA. Use innovation in his teaching\nB. Implement interactive teaching strategies\nC. Study the life of the Filipino heroes\nD. Continue professional education\nAnswer: C. There is no better way to understand a nation’s culture and heritage\nthan to study the life of its heroes. Heroes at all times manifest loyalty, patriotism, and\nthe love for the country. Truly, they are the epitome of what nationalism is all about.\n24. When all other requirements are met, when should religion is taught in public\nschools as provided for in the Constitution?\nA. Outside regular class hours\nB. Once a week during lunch break\nC. Once a week after class hours\nD. Within the regular class hours\nAnswer: D. Within the regular class hours - Article XIV of the 1987 Constitution\non Education, Science and Technology, Arts, Culture and Sports, Section 3 states that\n“Optional religious instruction shall be allowed to be taught to children in the public\nelementary and high school WITHIN the REGULAR CLASS HOURS by instructors",
