    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills (Part 4)",
    "body": "Source: 5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nB. Write lesson plans\nC. Conduct dialogue/conference with parents\nD. Clean the school garden\nAnswer: D. Cleaning the school garden is not part of the job description for a\nteacher. It is for the maintenance personnel.\n39. Who among the following philosophers is against the “banking concept of\neducation”?\nA. Paulo Freire\nC. Allan Bloom\nB. Neil Noddings\nD. Jerome Bruner\nAnswer: A. Paulo Freire. The “banking concept of education” viewed students as an\nempty account that needs to be filled by the teacher. Paulo Freire seriously attacked\nthis concept.\n40. A father tells his daughter “You are woman. You are meant for the hoe and so for\nyou, going to school is not necessary.” Is the father CORRECT?\nA. It depends on the place where the daughter and father live.\nB. Yes, women are meant to be mothers only.\nC. No, today women can take the job of men.\nD. No, there is gender equality in education.\nAnswer: D. No, there is gender equality in education. The situation is common to\nthose who do not believe in gender equality. Education is all. Everyone is entitled to\nhave it.\n41. For Aristotle, what aspect(s) of the subjects taught must be given emphasis:\nA. Practical aspect\nB. Theoretical aspect\nC. Practical and theoretical aspect\nD. One of the above\nAnswer: C. Practical and Theoretical aspect. A balance between the theoretical\nand practical aspects according to Aristotle, must be given emphasis in the subjects\nbeing taught.\n42. Who chaired the report “Learning the Treasure Within,” published by the\nUNESCO in 1996, by the International Commission in Education for the 21 st\ncentury? This advocates four pillars of education.\nA. Edgar Faure\nC. Harry Broudy\nB. Paolo Freire\nD. Jacques Delors\n\nAnswer: D. He chaired the report on Learning the Treasure Within.\n43. What does the acronym EFA imply for schools?\nA. The acceptance of exclusive schools for boys and girls.\nB. The stress on the superiority of formal education over that of alternative\nlearning systems\nC. Practice of inclusive education\nD. The concentration of formal education system.\nAnswer: C. Practice of inclusive education. This is one of the main facets of EFA\nor education for all, to make education accessible to all.\n44. This law express the mandate that the state “shall establish, maintain and support\na complete, adequate, and integrated system of education.”\nA. RA 7722\nC. 1987 Constitution\nB. RA 9155\nD. BP Blg. 232\nAnswer: C. 1987 Constitution. It mandates that the government shall establish,\nmaintain and support a complete, adequate and integrated system of education.\n45. This government agency is tasked to identify “centers for excellence” in program\nareas needed for the development of world-class scholarship, nation-building and\ndevelopment.\nA. DepEd\nC. TESDA\nB. CHED\nD. PAASCU\nAnswer: B. CHED is responsible for identifying Centers of Excellence or\nCENTEX.\n46. What are the necessary skills needed under the “learning to live together”\nA. Communication, self-control and handling emotions\nB. Interpretation of behaviors, problem solving and critical thinking\nC. Relationship building and cooperation, negotiation and meditation\nD. All of the above\nAnswer: D. A range of skills is necessary for learning to live together. It includes\nthe skills for self-control, handling emotions, communication, and critical thinking,\namong others.\n47. Which of the following is not a characteristic of the Child Friendly School System?\nA. Child-centered\nB. Gender-sensitive\nC. Promotes good health\n\nD. Instills love of country\nAnswer: D. Child Friendly School System (CFSS), which is initiated by the Philippine\nGovernment and UNICEF mandates that education should be gender-sensitive and not\ndiscriminating, child-centered, and promotes good health. Love of country is not\nmentioned in the CFSS.\n48. Professor Jerick Tan tends to appoint male students as group leaders. Which of\nthe following biases best applies to Professor Tan’s habit?\nA. Group bias\nB. Work bias\nC. Result bias\nD. Gender bias\nAnswer: D. Gender bias is a person’s preference toward a particular gender. This\nis not a good attribute of a teacher.\n49. Which of the following situations best depict the role of politics in influencing\neducational institutions as agents of change in society?\nI.\nFilipino students were forced to adopt in English as a medium of instruction\nduring the American regime.\nII.\nFilipino students had to learn Japanese during Japanese occupation.\nIII.\nFilipino students were not allowed to use English during Japanese\noccupation.\nA. III only\nB. I and II\n\nC. II and III\nD. I, II, and III\n\nAnswer: D. All situations depict that politics influences educational institution as\nagents of change.\n50. This educational program promotes among students belonging to a multi-cultural\nand inter-faith classroom.\nA. Indigenous development\nC. Multicultural education\nB. B. Sectoral solidification\nD. Cultural immersion\nAnswer: C. Multicultural education promotes equality among students from\nvarious beliefs and backgrounds.\n51. “Man by nature is good” according to Confucius, Rousseau and Mencius, this\ncharacteristic of man makes him easier to be _____.\nA. Motivated\nB. Taught\n\nC. Rewarded\nD. Respected\nAnswer: B. Taught. The innate goodness in man is an indication of his being\nobedient. He follows because he wants to learn. Motivation, reward and respect are just\nmanifestations of learning.\n52. Which among the following are the basic assumptions of behaviourists?\nI.\nAll behaviours are shaped by environmental events.\nII.\nMan is free and his behaviour can never be shaped by environment.\nIII.\nThe mind of a new born child is ‘tabula rasa’.\nA. I only\nB. II only\n\nC. III only\nD. All of the above.",
    "material_type": "notes",
    "is_premium": false
  },
  {
