    "title": "LET Social Studies Major: Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills (Part 9)",
    "body": "Source: 5. Social Dimensions of Education Q&A Drills.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nAnswer: A. Respect for authority requires the teachers to be careful with the\nstatements he/she tells about superiors especially in front of his/her students.\n96. Which of the following is an integral part of the teaching process?\nA. Teachers and principal\nC. Classroom management\nB. Lesson plan\nD. Rubric\nAnswer: C. Classroom management\n97. Which of the following is the Fundamental Moral Principle?\nA. Stealing is wrong.\nC. Do good and avoid evil.\nB. Health is wealth.\nD. Cheating is immoral.\nAnswer: C. “Do good and avoid evil”.\n98. “Moral example has greater effect on pupils’ discipline than laws ans codes of\npunishment” is an advice for teacher from _____.\nA. Confucius\nC. Lao Tzu\nB. Mohammed\nD. Mencius\nAnswer: A. Confucius - he considered the first and greatest teacher in China and\nis famous for his GOLDEN RULE he was noted for his political philosophy, stressing\nthat a leader or ruler governs not by laws or force but by virtue.\n99. According to Max Scheler’s Hierarchy of Values, ____ is the highest form of\nvalues.\nA. Pleasure values\nC. Spiritual values\nB. Vital values\nD. Values of the Holy\nAnswer: D. Values of the holy. This is the highest in max Scheler’s Hierarchy of\nValues.\n100.\nChristian’s morality, which guides their behaviour, dealing with moral\nissues is based on the _____.\nA. The eight fold path\nB. Five pillars\nC. Ten Commandments\nD. Christian Ethics\nAnswer: C. The Ten Commandments. The Ten Commandments is the basic\nfoundation of the Christian morality.\n\n101.\nYou want to report on a colleague’s act of immorality. You don’t have the\ncourage to confront her. To end the illicit affair with a married man, you write and\nsecretly distributes copies of your anonymous letter against your fellow teacher.\nWhat should have been done instead?\nA. Ask a third party to write the anonymous letter to prevent yourself from being\ninvolved.\nB. Talk to the married man with whom she is having an illicit affair.\nC. If the charge is valid; present such charge under oath before your school head.\nD. Secretly give anonymous letter only to the people concerned.\nAnswer: C. If the charge is valid; present such charge under oath before your\nschool head, in line with RA 7836 known as Philippine Teacher Professionalization Act\nof 1994, which states:\nSection 1\nTeachers shall, at all times, be imbued with the spirit of professional loyalty,\nmutual confidence, and faith in one another, self-sacrifice for the common good; and full\ncooperation with colleagues. When the best interest of the learners, the school, or the\nprofession is at stake in any controversy, teachers shall support one another.\nSection 2\nA teacher shall hold inviolate all confidential information concerning associates\nand the school, and shall not divulge to anyone documents which has not been officially\nreleased, or remove records from files without permission.\nTRUST and CONFIDENCE to the Teacher.\nSection 3\nIt shall be the responsibility of every teacher to seek correctives for what may\nappear to be an unprofessional and unethical conduct of any associate. However, this\nmay be done only if there is incontrovertible evidence for such conduct.\nSection 4\nA teacher may submit to the proper authorities any justifiable criticism against an\nassociate, preferably in writing, without violating the right of the individual concerned.\n102.\nTeacher Kris, a teacher for forty years, refuses to attend seminars. She\nclaims that her forty years of teaching is more than all the seminars she is asked\nto attend. Are her actuation and thinking in accordance with the Code of Ethics for\nProfessional Teachers?\nA. No, non-attendance to seminars means no professional growth.\nB. No, a professional teacher, regardless of teaching experience, ought to go\nthrough continuing process of education.\nC. Yes, because she taught for forty years and may have mastered the trade.\nD. Yes, provided she has an excellent performance rating.\nAnswer: B. No, a professional teacher, regardless of teaching experience, ought\nto go through continuing process of education.\n\n103.\nA principal asked her good teachers to write modular lesson in Social\nScience, then she had them published with her name printed as author. Which is\nunethical in this case?\nA. She was exclusive beneficiary of the royalty from the modules.\nB. She had modular lessons published when they worth publishing.\nC. She got the merit which was due for his teacher-writers.\nD. She burdened the teachers with work not related to teaching.\nAnswer: C. She got the merit which was due for his teacher-writers. A teacher\nshall live with dignity in all places at all times. Furthermore, a teacher shall place\npremium upon self-respect and self-discipline as the principle of personal behaviour in\nall relationships with other and in all situations. Therefore, a teacher should practice\njustice by giving what is due to others.\n104.\nIn a faculty recollection, the teachers were asked to share their thoughts of\nthe learner, their primary customer.\nTeacher A said this: “The learner is the product of his environment. Sometimes he\nhas no choice. He is determined by his environment.”\nTeacher B: The learner can choose what he can become despite his environment.\nTeacher C: the learner is a social being who learns well through an active\ninterplay with others.\nTeacher D: the learner is a rational being. Schools should develop his rational and\nmoral powers.\nWhose philosophical concept is that of Teacher A’s?\nA. Rationalist’s\nC. Existentialist’s\nB. Behaviorist’s\nD. Progressivist’s\nAnswer: B. Behaviorist. A behaviourist believes that human can be best explained\nin terms of responses to external stimuli. Also, education can be best achieved by\nmodifying or changing student behaviours in a socially acceptable manner through the\narrangement of the conditions of learning. The control is obtained not by manipulating\nthe individual, but the environment.\n105.\n“Pleasure is not the highest good.” This teaching is against what\nphilosophy?\nA. Realism\nB. Essentialism\nC. Epicureanism\nD. Empiricism\nAnswer: C. Epicureanism. “Eat, drink and be merry for tomorrow you will die.” This\nis known as the Pleasure Principle of the Epicureans.",
