    "body": "Source: SOCIAL SCIENCE PART 10.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nIX. Social Studies Pedagogy\n41. The inquiry approach encourages learners to:\nA. Memorize facts\nB. Ask and investigate questions\nC. Listen passively\nD. Follow the teacher’s opinion\n42. A Social Studies teacher who integrates current issues with lessons is promoting:\nA. Interdisciplinary learning\nB. Values clarification\nC. Critical thinking\nD. Historical perspective\n43. The use of community immersion as a teaching method is an example of:\nA. Experiential learning\nB. Constructivism\nC. Both A and B\nD. Lecture-based method\n44. The best strategy for teaching controversial issues is:\nA. Debate\nB. Role-play\nC. Lecture\nD. Dictation\n45. A rubric-based project in Social Studies encourages:\nA. Subjectivity\nB. Performance assessment\nC. Objective testing\nD. Guessing\n\nX. Research and Assessment\n46. Historical sources that are secondhand are called:\nA. Primary sources\nB. Secondary sources\nC. Tertiary sources\nD. Authentic documents\n47. Quantitative research mainly deals with:\nA. Opinions and feelings\nB. Words and meanings\nC. Numbers and statistics\nD. Interviews\n48. Which is the best evaluation tool for measuring long-term projects?\nA. Rubric\nB. Quiz\nC. Oral exam\nD. True/False test\n\nUltimate LET Reviewer (2025-2026)\nLearnUP PH Reviewer\n49. When a teacher assesses students’ map-making activity, the best type of assessment is:\nA. Authentic assessment\nB. Objective test\nC. Multiple-choice exam\nD. Essay writing\n50. The goal of action research in Social Studies is to:\nA. Discover new theories\nB. Improve classroom practices\nC. Create national policies\nD. Test new scientific laws\n\nUltimate LET Reviewer (2025-2026)\nLearnUP PH Reviewer\n\nAnswer Key with Short Explanations\n1. Tydings-McDuffie Act - Created the Philippine Commonwealth.\n2. Educated Filipino elites - Ilustrados were reformist intellectuals.\n3. Friendship and alliance - Symbol of early diplomacy.\n4. The Propaganda Movement - Inspired by martyrdom of Gomburza.\n5. Article III - Establishes religious freedom.\n6. The English King - Magna Carta limited monarchy.\n7. World War I - Triggered by assassination of Archduke Ferdinand.\n8. Communism - Philosophy of Karl Marx.\n9. Reason and individual rights - Core of Enlightenment.\n10. The U.S. did not join - Weakened League of Nations.\n11. Volcanic and earthquake zone - The Pacific Ring of Fire.\n12. A and C - Turkey and Kazakhstan are transcontinental.\n13. Istanbul - City straddles Europe and Asia.\n14. Indonesia - World’s largest archipelago.\n15. Topographic map - Shows elevation and landforms.\n16. Cultures should be understood within their context - Cultural relativism.\n17. Family - Primary social group.\n18. Enculturation - Passing culture to new generations.\n19. Socially prohibited acts - Meaning of taboos.\n20. Mother’s line - Matrilineal descent.\n21. Abuse of authority - Separation of powers prevents tyranny.\n22. 24 - Total senators in the Philippines.\n23. Judicial review - Supreme Court’s constitutional power.\n24. Local Government Code - Source of LGU powers.\n25. Suffrage - Right to vote.\n26. Increases - Law of supply.\n27. Value of next best alternative - Opportunity cost definition.\n28. Taxation and spending - Fiscal policy tools.\n29. Imports and exports - Defines balance of trade.\n30. Individual markets - Focus of microeconomics.\n31. Globalization - Outsourcing is its outcome.\n32. Unequal access to technology - Digital divide.\n33. Millennium Development Goals - Replaced by SDGs.\n34. Climate change and carbon emissions - Focus of Kyoto Protocol.\n35. IMF - Promotes global financial stability.\n36. Ifugao - Home of the Banaue Rice Terraces.\n37. Mt. Apo - Highest mountain in the Philippines.\n38. Amihan and Habagat - Two monsoon systems.\n39. Cagayan River - Longest river.\n40. Southeast Asia - Regional grouping of the Philippines.\n41. Ask and investigate questions - Inquiry-based learning.\n42. Critical thinking - Integrating issues develops reasoning.\n43. Both A and B - Immersion applies experiential constructivism.\n44. Debate - Best for controversial topics.\n45. Performance assessment - Rubrics measure outputs.\n46. Secondary sources - Derived from primary accounts.\n47. Numbers and statistics - Focus of quantitative studies.\n48. Rubric - Evaluates complex performance.\n49. Authentic assessment - Evaluates real-world task.\n50. Improve classroom practices - Purpose of action research.",
